findings.description:
The header and the strip_heredocs docstring say only heredoc BODIES are removed before the path-word scan (1.2.2, DEC-0541). The regex `<<-?\s*(['"]?)(word)\1[^
]*
(?:.*?
)*?\s*\2…` consumes `[^
]*
`, i.e. everything after the delimiter on the operator line, and the whole match is replaced by `<<HEREDOC`. Redirect targets and arguments placed after the operator are therefore dropped from `body` before ABS_WORD and HOME_WORD run. `cat <<'EOF' > /root/notes.txt`, `cat <<EOF > /mnt/data/x`, and in host mode `cat <<'EOF' > ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md` or `> $HOME/.config/x` are all scanned as `cat <<HEREDOC` and produce no outside_path hit. `cmd <<EOF > path` is the standard way an agent writes a file from Bash, so this is a deterministic detection loss on a very common command shape, not an edge case.

findings.suggested_fix:
Strip only the body: match the operator line but keep it, e.g. capture `(<<-?\s*(['"]?)(\w+)\2[^
]*
)` as group and substitute with `\1<<HEREDOC
`, or split the regex so `[^
]*` is retained in the replacement. Add a bats case for `cat <<'EOF' > /root/x
body
EOF` (container) and `> ~/.claude/x` (host) expecting outside_path.
